    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>The photo on your phone is personal data. So is the name on the job sheet. UK GDPR applies from the moment you collect it. Here is what to do.</p><p><strong>This page is general information, not legal advice.</strong> Ask a solicitor, or the Information Commission at <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk\">ico.org.uk</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>Most field reports hold personal data. Names, addresses, photos.</li><li>Almost all commercial firms must register with the Information Commission. £40 a year for a small organisation.</li><li>Pick a lawful basis. Contract or legitimate interests. Rarely consent.</li><li>Keep data no longer than you need. Write a retention policy, even one page.</li><li>Where data is hosted matters. EU hosting meets UK and EU rules.</li><li>Tell people what you collect. A short notice is enough.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "What counts",
      "heading": "Your reports hold personal data",
      "html": "<p>Personal data is anything about a person who can be identified. A name. An address linked to a person. A photo where someone is recognisable.</p><p>The format does not matter. Paper, PDF, cloud photo or voice note.</p><p>Common examples: a householder's address, an occupier's signature, names of staff, photos with people in them. Data about a company is usually not personal data. A sole trader under their own name is the usual exception.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Step one",
      "heading": "Register with the Information Commission. It takes ten minutes",
      "html": "<p>Most organisations that handle personal data must pay the Information Commission's yearly fee. For small organisations it is £40 a year.</p><p>Few are exempt. Most sole traders and small firms doing commercial work must register. <strong>Not registering is a criminal offence.</strong></p><p>The Information Commission has a self-check at <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/data-protection-fee/\">ico.org.uk</a>. Registering is only the start. The other rules apply either way.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Step two",
      "heading": "Pick a lawful basis. Skip consent",
      "html": "<p>You need a lawful basis for everything you do with personal data.</p><p><strong>Contract.</strong> You need the data to do the job for your client. That covers a client's name and address.</p><p><strong>Legitimate interests.</strong> You need it for your own proper aims, and it does not harm the person. Safety, compliance and admin records usually fit.</p><p>Consent is rarely right for field data. It is hard to rely on, and people can withdraw it.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Step three",
      "heading": "Keep it no longer than you need",
      "html": "<p>There is no one number for field reports. It depends on why you made the report.</p><ul><li><strong>Legal records</strong> such as RIDDOR, gas safety and EICR have their own minimum times. Keep them at least that long.</li><li><strong>Contract records</strong>: usually six years, or twelve if made by deed.</li><li><strong>Safety records</strong>: the legal minimum if there is one, or six years.</li></ul><p>A one-page retention policy shows you thought about it.</p>"
    },
    {
